{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach and findings so far. Batches 1-2 were an unbiased survey of every base feature across all legal lookbacks, to locate effects before any optimization. Findings: (a) strong short-horizon cross-sectional REVERSAL - ret(3..10) SR about -1.0 to -1.2 with matching negative IC, mirrored by ma(n) and by distance-above-rolling-min; (b) a positive volatility premium in SR but negative in IC, meaning the vol effect is tail concentrated rather than broad; (c) a low-kurtosis premium, neg(kurt(250)) SR +0.96 IC +0.012; (d) a modest negative-skew premium. Long-horizon momentum has positive IC but negative SR and is not usable. Batches 3-6 refined these. Two enhancements generalize across the whole parameter grid rather than at isolated points: dividing a signed signal by trailing vol (a proper risk adjustment) and time-series z-scoring the raw feature before use. Both lift the reversal leg from SR 1.2 to about 1.9-2.0 uniformly across lookbacks. Batch 8 was a deliberate falsification test. The reversal signals collapse to SR about 0.0-0.3 when delayed 5 days, which is exactly the decay profile a genuine short-horizon reversal must have and rules out a look-ahead artifact; the kurtosis-per-unit-vol signal survives a 20-day delay, confirming it is a slow characteristic rather than a timing signal. cs_rank of a signal reproduces its SR exactly, so the scorer is rank based and monotone wrappers are no-ops - only the ordering matters, which is why div and mul interactions change results while sign preserving rescalings do not. Selection philosophy for the holdout: with several hundred expressions tested the single highest train SR is heavily selection biased, so I am choosing from PLATEAUS - regions where every neighbouring parameterization scores similarly - and preferring signals whose SR and rank IC agree in sign, since IC agreement indicates broad cross-sectional breadth rather than a few lucky days. Equal-weight cs_z blends of the two economically distinct legs reach train SR 2.6 with no fitted weights, and the entire blend surface sits in 2.35-2.65, which is plateau behaviour rather than a spike.", "ts": "2026-09-01T06:57:43.787155+00:00"}
